  "harness_commit": "0c5e6a09da",
  "measurement_scope": "Lite write-amplification counting harness (zig build lite-write-amplification, Debug), run on perf/lwa-test-infra (off perf/lwa-integration) after making the 8-writer modes use real, barrier-synchronized concurrent threads and adding the missing primary-only 8-writer mode. Operation counts (durable syncs, write/pwrite calls and bytes, durable commits), not timing, keyed by Stage and attributed to the thread that issued the update (calling_thread) vs any other thread (background). primary_only[_8_writers] deletes the default full_text_index_v0 index and uses sync_level=write; indexed[_8_writers] keeps it and uses sync_level=full_index, which blocks until full-text is visible. 128 single-doc updates total, 1 KiB changing text per update (matching scripts/bench/lite_sqlite_comparison.py's workload); the 8-writer modes split those 128 updates across 8 OS threads, each barrier-synchronized to arrive at the single-writer admission queue together on every one of its 16 rounds.",
  "known_limitations": [
    "artifact_pin, lsm_wal, progress, status_snapshot and replay_retirement all read zero for this workload: the default full_text_index_v0 index's segments stay small enough that PersistentIndex.indexSegmentOwned never takes the segment-file-backed/segment-WAL branch (segment_files/uses_segment_wal), so those stage-tagged scopes are simply never entered. The 'other' bucket (both calling-thread and background) is correspondingly large and still unattributed; see docs/design/lite-performance/README.md.",
    "maintenance_lease_acquired and serving_runtime_refresh read zero: the harness opens Lite directly through the capi.openLiteHandleWithRuntime embedding seam with a .manual BackendRuntime, bypassing capi/lite_connection.zig's Owner/connection-pool layer where those two counters are hooked.",
    "mean_primary_group_size reads 1.0 for primary_only_8_writers and 0.5 for indexed_8_writers (identical to the matching single-writer mode's structural 1x/2x-commits-per-update shape), even with a barrier forcing all 8 writer threads to arrive at the admission queue together on every round. This is not a measurement bug: lite-single-commit-write-path.md's \"throughput falls as writers are added\" note already documents why -- the writer slot, the DB apply lock and the per-call cross-process path lease are all held across the fsync, so the existing 64-request group-commit queue in submitScopedMutation cannot yet bundle concurrent foreground writers' commits regardless of how they arrive. This harness now measures that honestly instead of masking it with accidental non-contention (the pre-fix harness spawned real OS threads but never forced them to overlap, so it reported these same numbers by coincidence, not by proof). mean_primary_group_size should rise above 1.0 once that slot-held-across-fsync limitation is fixed; the ceiling gate protects the 8-writer modes from regressing in the meantime.",
    "indicative_writes_per_second_debug is a Debug build, single-machine, 128-update measurement and is sensitive to build mode, machine load and scheduling, unlike every other field in this report; it is not a throughput guarantee. Use scripts/bench/lite_sqlite_comparison.py for a throughput/latency comparison under contention."
  ],
  "receipt_comparison": "Against write-amplification-baseline.json (measured at origin/main@6c0b004e95): primary-only calling-thread syncs/update fell from 3.000 to 2.000 and indexed fell from 66.844 to 32.563 on this integration branch -- the single-commit write path work (WS1-WS8) landing here. The 8-writer modes match their single-writer counterparts exactly in both the baseline and here, for the different reasons each receipt's known_limitations explains: the baseline's harness never forced contention, so the match was not evidence of anything; this harness now forces real contention every round and the match is evidence that the write path does not yet turn that contention into fewer commits.",
